The '''History of Sarta (1609–1705)''' covers the period from the establishment of the [[Sarta Colony of Guajicutea]] to the treaty that ended the [[Sarta–Volisania War]] in 1705. It was a period of colonial consolidation, maritime expansion, and rising economic strain. The Sartan crown moved its principal court from [[Cinepolis]] in the northern highlands to [[Bahia Alta]] on the coast, and later returned the court to [[Cinepolis]] after the loss of the coast in the final phase of the war. The kingdom's political centre increasingly turned toward sea traffic, overseas administration, and the management of [[Guajicutea]].

== Colonial consolidation ==

The conquest of Guajicutea in 1609 gave Sarta its first permanent overseas possession. The new colony ended Guaji kingship and replaced the existing feudal order with Sartan colonial administration. In the colony's early years, the Sartan state relied on provincial governors, church officials, and military detachments to secure the island and to prevent a restoration of local power.

The crown's decision to move its principal court from Cinepolis to Bahia Alta reflected the new political geography of the kingdom. Cinepolis remained important as an inland highland centre, but Bahia Alta became the main seat of royal administration, customs collection, and maritime coordination. The move brought the crown closer to shipping routes and to the colonial networks that now linked mainland Sarta, Guajicutea, and the wider Capuyaquiran region.

== Social and administrative change ==

The colonial period strengthened the role of literate officials, merchants, shipwrights, and ecclesiastical administrators. Sarta's administration became more specialised as the kingdom managed a growing overseas domain. Port towns gained influence, while the coastal regions became more commercially oriented and more closely tied to maritime traffic than the inland highlands.

The new colonial order also altered social relations. Sartan officials and settlers increasingly distinguished between the mainland kingdom and its overseas possession, while Guajicutea developed into a major site of administration, plantation growth, and migration. The colony remained formally subordinate to the crown, but it acquired its own local institutions and an increasingly distinct colonial society.

== Ports, shipyards, and trade ==

Maritime development was one of the defining features of the period. The crown encouraged shipbuilding, naval provisioning, and port expansion, especially after the move to Bahia Alta. Sartan seafaring depended on a growing network of coastal officials, merchants, and shipwrights, and it increasingly tied the kingdom's internal politics to overseas trade.

Guajicutea became central to this system. Trade with the island expanded, and the colony's ports became points of contact between Sarta and the wider world. Over time, the colony's agricultural output, ship repair, and customs revenues made it one of the most important parts of Sarta's overseas economy.

== Church and language policy ==

The colonial administration treated church and language policy as tools of rule. Petran Catholic clergy took over the formal religious life of the colony after the expulsion of earlier missions tied to Burawa and Volisania. Sartan became the language of administration, trade, and religious conformity, even though local populations continued to use their own languages in daily life.

This policy did not create complete linguistic uniformity, but it placed pressure on the island's population and encouraged the spread of Sartan in public institutions. The result was a more integrated colonial elite, even as local identities remained strong in villages, districts, and port communities.

== War of the Mesogean ==

The [[War of the Mesogean]] between [[Burawa]] and [[Volisania]] gave Sarta a wider strategic opening. The war was fought primarily in the Old World, while the Capuyaquiran became a secondary theatre of naval harassment and impressment. In Guajicutea, both colonial powers reduced their attention as the conflict continued, leaving Sarta room to expand its influence.

Sarta used a combination of diplomacy, aid, and pressure to isolate Guajicutea from its previous colonial protectors. An emissary arrived in Tabakurí in 1600, and after the hurricane of 1601 the Sartan crown provided substantial relief. In 1605, Sarta and Guajicutea formed a formal alliance. In 1607, Sarta purchased the Burawi fort at Waribá, and in 1608 it reached a neutrality arrangement with Volisania that brought the Volisanian garrison out of the island. By late 1608, Sarta had become the only external power with a substantial presence in Guajicutea.

The brief [[Sarta-Guajicutea War]] of 1608–1609 followed, ending with the capture of Tabakurí and the deposition of [[Boqor Carlos I]]. The result was the establishment of the Sarta Colony of Guajicutea, which became the core of Sarta's overseas system.

== Later colonial development ==

By the middle decades of the seventeenth century, Sarta's colonial order had become more settled. The normalisation of relations with Burawa in 1634 restored trade and reduced the threat of sudden intervention. In 1638, the [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] was founded, and in 1642 construction began on the [[Castillo de San Esteban]], completed in 1656. These projects reinforced Waribá's role as a shipbuilding and military centre.

The colony also continued to grow demographically and economically. Plantation agriculture expanded, merchant families accumulated influence, and the administrative separation between mainland Sarta and Guajicutea became more pronounced. Bahia Alta remained the main mainland political centre, but Guajicutea increasingly shaped the kingdom's foreign relations and commercial priorities.

== Sarta–Volisania War ==

The [[Sarta–Volisania War]] began in 1683 and lasted until 1705. It was fought largely outside the Capuyaquiran, but its effects were felt strongly in Sarta and Guajicutea. Volisania launched the war as a preemptive strike, fearing that Sarta's colonial expansion and maritime strength would continue to grow. The Sartan coast also contained a sizable Volisanian population, especially in the coastal towns, and many of those communities were sympathetic to Volisania, in contrast to the more inland Sartan homeland. As Volisania lost access to coastal trade and naval routes, Sarta faced a prolonged period of economic strain, unpaid officials, and military overextension. During the war, Volisania captured [[Bahia Alta]] and annexed much of the Sartan coast. Guajicutea was turned into a fortified colonial base, but the wider colonial economy deteriorated as trade declined.

The war also altered political life in the colony. Officials and merchants struggled with taxation, shortages, and shipping losses, while the crown relied more heavily on the colony's shipyards and port infrastructure. The loss of Bahia Alta forced the court to return inland to [[Cinepolis]], and the strain sharpened the distinction between the imperial ambitions of Sarta and the practical limits of its resources.

== Treaty of Bahia Alta ==

The Treaty of Bahia Alta ended the war and confirmed Sarta's survival as an overseas colonial power, but it also exposed the kingdom's weakness. Guajicutea remained in Sartan hands, yet the [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] was ceded to the [[Companyia Reial del Capuyaquiran]], reducing direct royal control over the colony's principal industrial asset. The settlement cut Sarta off from direct access to the sea, while allowing ships to move through Volisanian territory via the Sarta river system. It left Sarta with a possession that was more autonomous, more expensive to defend, and more deeply tied to colonial trade than before.

The period ended with Sarta stronger at sea than it had been in the sixteenth century, but also more dependent on colonial institutions, inland strongholds, and regulated river transit. The return of the court to Cinepolis marked the end of the coastal phase of the war and the beginning of a more defensive posture in the kingdom's political centre.

